    {
      "title": "Honorifics and Formality",
      "explanation": "Korean has formal and informal speech levels. Formal endings (-습니다/-ㅂ니다) are used with strangers, elders, and in professional settings. Informal endings (-아요/-어요) are used with peers. Very informal speech drops these endings.",
      "examples": ["Formal: 먹습니다 (meoknseumnida = eat)", "Polite: 먹어요 (meogeoyo)", "Informal: 먹어 (meogeo)"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Particles",
      "explanation": "Korean uses particles (postpositions) to mark grammatical roles, similar to Japanese. 이/가 marks the subject, 을/를 marks the object, 에 marks location/direction.",
      "examples": ["저는 (jeoneun) = I (topic)", "사과를 (sagwareul) = apple (object)", "학교에 (hakgyoe) = to school"]
